<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeongju</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seorabeol, Geumseong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeongju is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> that was the capital of Silla for nearly a thousand years, until 935. It is rich in royal tombs, temples and palace sites, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seokguram">Seokguram</a> grotto and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae">Cheomseongdae</a> observatory, and its historic areas are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. It is often called a museum without walls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyeongju.jpg" alt="Gyeongju" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kyoww (montage) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyeongju_montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…politics of the Three Kingdoms: Silla was for a time a near-protectorate, as the stele and a bronze vessel inscribed with Gwanggaeto's name found in a Silla tomb in <b>Gyeongju</b> attest, while Baekje's long rivalry with Goguryeo drove its alliances with the southern Chinese courts and Yamato Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Munmu, who completed Silla's unification, is said to have asked for burial at sea so that he could become a dragon guarding Korea; a rock islet off the coast near <b>Gyeongju</b> is held to be his tomb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 683 Anseung was summoned to <b>Gyeongju</b>, given the Silla royal surname Kim and settled there.</blockquote><a href="/state/bodeok">Bodeok</a></li><li><blockquote>Jinhan formed in the 1st century BCE in the <b>Gyeongju</b> and Daegu area east of the Nakdong river.</blockquote><a href="/state/jinhan">Jinhan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 927 Gyeon Hwon sacked the Silla capital at <b>Gyeongju</b> and forced King Gyeongae to kill himself, then defeated Wang Geon of Goryeo near Daegu.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a></li></ul></section>
